Code Reusability: Subclasses (derived classes) automatically inherit attributes and methods from the superclass (base class). 
                  This allows you to write common logic once and reuse it across multiple classes without duplicating code.

Maintainability and Modularity: Updating or fixing a bug in the base class automatically updates that functionality across all derived classes, making the code much easier to maintain and refactor.

Extensibility: You can easily add new features or subclasses to an existing system without modifying the original base code, following the Open/Closed Principle.

Polymorphism Support: Inheritance lays the foundation for runtime polymorphism (method overriding), allowing you to treat objects of different derived classes as objects of a single base class type.
